WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:03.960
- [Narrator] Most people know helium

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.840
as the lighter than air gas
that fills our party balloons.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.510
But more importantly it's
an irreplaceable element

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:13.663
for science and industry.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.560
Helium was discovered in
1868 during a solar eclipse.

00:00:21.734 --> 00:00:26.734
Astronomers observed a yellow
light in the sun's corona

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.840
and couldn't explain it
with any known elements

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.400
at the time.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.741
Their observations turned
out to be the first sightings

00:00:33.741 --> 00:00:38.610
of a new element and given
its discovery near the sun

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:42.100
this element was named after
the Greek god of the sun,

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.153
Helios.

00:00:44.045 --> 00:00:47.280
Helium is the second lightest element.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.750
Second only to hydrogen.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:52.570
It's located near the
top of the periodic table

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:54.850
coming in at atomic number two.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:58.130
Meaning it has two protons in its core.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:00.420
It's usually found in gas form

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:05.073
but turns into liquid below
negative 452 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:11.403
Helium is the second most
abundant element in the universe.

00:01:11.403 --> 00:01:14.100
Created during the Big Bang

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:19.100
and found in stars, planets,
and even our own moon.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.563
However it's relatively rare on earth.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.620
On our planet, helium forms
as a gaseous byproduct

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:32.250
of ancient uranium that's
decayed over billions of years

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.960
and is trapped beneath
the earth's surface.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.277
It's harvested from natural gas reserves

00:01:37.277 --> 00:01:39.423
and can't be artificially produced

00:01:39.423 --> 00:01:43.453
making helium a non-renewable
resource on our planet.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.503
Helium has a variety of applications

00:01:48.503 --> 00:01:51.563
largely due to its unique characteristics.

00:01:52.486 --> 00:01:55.090
It's a noble gas so it's
less likely to interact

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.090
with other elements.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:01.430
When mixed with neon, helium
can create low power lasers

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:04.193
used to scan barcodes
at checkout counters.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.289
When mixed with oxygen in
a high pressure environment

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:11.670
helium allows deep sea
divers to breath in the air

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.973
from their oxygen tanks more easily.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.863
And since helium has a low boiling point,

00:02:17.863 --> 00:02:20.747
it's useful in extremely
cold temperatures.

00:02:20.747 --> 00:02:24.930
Because of this it can cool
some of the most powerful

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.706
electromagnets on earth

00:02:26.706 --> 00:02:29.743
including the large Hadron Collider.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.250
While helium can be applied
in seemingly countless ways,

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:38.640
its availability is finite.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.560
Because it's lightweight
when helium is released

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.440
into the atmosphere it floats into space

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.473
and is lost to earth forever.

00:02:48.294 --> 00:02:51.211
(drumline playing)

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:56.730
But by pursuing avenues
of helium recycling,

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:59.060
exploration, and storage,

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.486
we may be able to protect and sustain

00:03:01.486 --> 00:03:05.053
this precious non-renewable resource.

